--- body ---
## Context
Unticketed prerequisite of 0026's golden gate (review-implementation.md §3,
a panel document now lost; DECISIONS.md, 2026-08-31): no pinned fixture corpus
exists — bench/queries.txt is 16 queries, and every
answer-set artifact was measured against the author's private library, which
cannot be committed. Needed: a committable multilingual document set, ~40
queries, pinned answer sets at k=10, and a harness that builds an index over
it WITHOUT Zotero. The thresholds the gate then enforces live once in
SPEC.md §5.2.8.

Rulings of 2026-09-02 (DECISIONS.md, "The golden fixture corpus") reshape the
work. What the corpus is: a source recipe (archive identifier, byte hash, item
metadata, licence basis per document), an injection script into a collection
of a public Zotero library, and a committed export of the items JSON and each
attachment's `/fulltext` JSON, replayed by the harness through 0026's mock
local API. The committed `text.txt` files of PR #151 are superseded by that
export: zoteus indexes Zotero's client extraction, and the probe measured
word-set Jaccard 0,39–0,61 between it and `pdftotext`, with a 100-page cap and
no page breaks. Where a document may come from: a public archive identifier,
never a personal library; versioned open archives (arXiv vN, Zenodo version
DOI, HAL id with version) beside Gallica, Internet Archive, Wikimedia Commons
and Wikisource revision ids; no self-deposit. Zotero #6012's calibration
corpus stays out.

## Reframe: one adversarial library, a derived question bank

Ratified 2026-09-03 (DECISIONS.md). This ticket's 25 exit criteria, below,
had accreted across roughly 14 independent dimensions with no declared
partition — the monster-ticket pattern. Reframed as one deliberately
adversarial library (personal-library-shaped fixture plus one closed group
under the same recipe — the group-library slice is folded in, not dropped)
plus a question bank sized to what the library's hard cases demand.

**The exit criteria below are now a coverage ledger, not the operational
spec.** Each bullet is satisfied by naming which question(s) exercise it; a
bullet with no citing question is a surfaced gap, not a silent drop.

**The tally, revised 2026-09-03 to exactly 200.** Each row now states what it
tests and a worked example, not a bare label; a `languages` column makes the
multilingual cross-cut explicit rather than folding it silently into
q/sub-case. `languages = 1` marks a row where a further multiply would
double-count — either because the row is already inherently about language
(SHOULD-language articles, cross-lingual anchor pairs), because its sub-cases
already name a specific script internally (Intersections), or because the row
is pinned to one already-chosen, single-language artifact (the Gutenberg
work — one specific English public-domain ebook, not a document-agnostic
dimension). `2` or `3` cross a document-agnostic row against that many
MUST-tier languages (EN/FR/VI) where the failure mode is plausibly script- or
locale-sensitive; on that test, extraction-cap-crossing's char-offset
arithmetic (multibyte UTF-8 diverges byte-count from char-count) earns its
`2` the same way structural marks and R33's probes do.

| dimension | tests | worked example | sub-cases | q/sub-case | languages | subtotal |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| SHOULD-language articles | SHOULD-tier language extraction/ranking without full commitment | "Find passage in an Arabic article" | 6 | 1 | 1 | 6 |
| Source format coverage | extraction survives authentic containers without conversion | "Find passage in EPUB" (EN and VI) | 9 | 1 | 2 | 18 |
| Work-identity relations | translation/duplicate pairs distinguished or related correctly | "Does the correct-language twin of a translation pair answer the query"; "Is a metadata-conflicting duplicate not silently merged with its twin" | 4 | 2 | 1 | 8 |
| Multi-attachment parent shapes | deterministic attachment selection among multiple candidates | "Find passage in the correct-language sibling attachment" | 3 | 2 | 1 | 6 |
| Failure controls | visible, not silent, degradation | "Query touches a bad PDF — does it fail loudly" (EN and VI) | 7 | 1 | 2 | 14 |
| Structural marks | a hit locates to the exact sub-unit | "Find passage in a table cell", "Find passage in a caption" (EN/FR/VI) | 6 | 2 | 3 | 36 |
| Annotation provenance/shape/edge cases | annotation attribution and edge cases | "Find a note surviving after its source was deleted" (EN/FR/VI) | 12 | 1 | 3 | 36 |
| Gutenberg cap-crossing pair | cap logic on a non-paginated plain-text work | "Find passage after char 600000" | 1 | 3 | 1 | 3 |
| Extraction-cap-crossing conditions | correctness on both sides of the stock caps | "Find passage at page 1000", "…after char 40000 / 200000" (EN and VI) | 3 | 2 | 2 | 12 |
| R33 probe shapes | ranking survives adversarial geometry | "Find passage via a rare exact string" / paraphrase / agreement case (EN/FR/VI) | 3 | 4 | 3 | 36 |
| Intersections (monster × script, scale × multilingual, cap-crossing × script, structural × scale) | compounding failure modes, each combo already naming its own script/scale so no further language multiply applies | "A monster document in Arabic script still resolves"; "The char-offset cap lands at the same passage in a multibyte script as in English" | 4 | 2 | 1 | 8 |
| Cross-lingual anchor pairs | EN/FR query surfaces untranslated VN content (R29) | "Find passage in VN content from an EN query" | 4 | 2 | 1 | 8 |
| Group-library slice | closed group behaves like the personal library | "Find passage in a closed-group-library item" (EN and VI) | 4 | 1 | 2 | 8 |
| Harness canary — a deliberate 15th row, not a rounding fudge: it tests the replay harness itself rather than any corpus dimension, so a failure here means "distrust every other row," not "the corpus has a gap" | the replay harness is wired correctly, independent of adversarial content | "Find a known trivial passage" | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| **total** | | | | | | **200** |

Item-type fidelity is corpus-composition bookkeeping (recorded per injected
item, not a dedicated question) and isn't in this table. This is a first-pass
estimate — refine per-row as real documents are chosen; a document satisfying
several dimensions at once may let the authored count run lower. The
`languages` cross was deliberately stopped at the 3 MUST-tier languages
(EN/FR/VI): running structural marks or boundary probes against the full
9-language roster would require sourcing table/footnote/caption-bearing
documents in Arabic, Russian, Chinese, German, Hindi and Spanish specifically
— a materially larger sourcing lift than the SHOULD-language row above
commits to, and this ticket's own log already flags SHOULD-tier sourcing as
the hard, sometimes-infeasible part. Raise a specific row's `languages` value
later, per SHOULD language, as that language's corpus gets built out.

**Reuse moves from a per-question ceiling to a per-document floor — a
replacement, not a strict inversion, since the two bounds constrain
different sides of the question↔document relation.** PR #315's ratified
bound was documents-per-question: "a question anchors to at most 4 documents
before a distinct source is required." This ruling retracts that and puts
the bound on the other axis instead: a document must anchor at least 5
questions before it's "spent," no upper bound on how many. Assuming each
question anchors to one primary document — the common case, though not
universal, since a question can still legitimately draw supporting context
from more than one — 200 questions at a ≥5-reuse floor need at most ~40
distinct documents (200/5); a question that anchors several documents at
once loosens this bound rather than breaking it, since it only adds
incidences without adding distinct documents. The practical effect is the
same direction either way: concentrating effort in authoring adversarial
questions against already-sourced hard cases rather than in further
sourcing. PR #315's anti-gaming rationale — stop a handful of flexible
documents from satisfying the tally by stacking unrelated dimensions onto
them — is explicitly retracted by this ruling, not overlooked; if reuse
concentration turns out to blunt the adversarial value of a document (the
same passage answering unrelated questions rather than genuinely different
hard cases), that is a fresh call for whoever authors the
question bank, not a violation of this bound.

**Sequencing is unchanged.** The harness triplet (injection script, export
snapshot, Zotero-free replay harness) goes first, against the 17
already-hashed documents — it does not wait on the 9 records stuck behind
browser-only challenge pages (4 HAL, 4 Gallica, 1 Malynes), and it lets the
question-bank design be tested against a real replay before more sourcing
hours are spent.

## Child 2's scope widened (ruling 2026-09-04)

Ratified 2026-09-04 (DECISIONS.md). Child 2 is bounded by what the
200-question tally needs present in the library, not by PR #151's stalled
re-sourcing table: closing the 4 HAL + 1 Malynes browser-only fetches is
necessary but not sufficient. Three further moves:

- **Gallica dropped as an admitted archive.** Every Gallica fetch attempted
  for this fixture answered a scripted client with an ALTCHA challenge — the
  archive's standing behaviour, not a transient block. Its four current
  records — cournot-1838-recherches, depitre-1908-oeuvres-cournot,
  des-michels-1883-luc-van-tien, des-michels-1884-kim-van-kieu-t2p1 — lose
  their source. The des Michels pair is the FR–VN cross-lingual anchor
  ruling 5 (2026-09-02) built for R29; losing it without a replacement
  removes that anchor shape, not just two records.
- **The mix rebalances toward articles**, fewer books relative to standalone
  articles. No ratio pinned — first-pass, adjustable, the posture already
  carried by the 200-count and the reuse floor.
- **Gutenberg (admitted 2026-09-03) and arXiv (admitted by ruling 3,
  2026-09-02, unused in the recipe so far) open for broader use; the
  Wikipedia constellation is newly admitted** — the article namespace
  itself, beside the already-admitted Wikisource and Commons, each article
  pinned by permanent revision id the same way a Wikisource entry already
  is. Archives are drawn on only as the tally's dimensions need them.

**Open, not ruled: EconLit.** Raised with the author's own hedge. To this
session's knowledge EconLit is an abstracting and indexing database —
bibliographic records and abstracts, not hosted full text — so it does not
obviously meet the admitted-archive definition (a public, third-party-hosted,
persistent identifier naming one fixed set of bytes). Carried open rather
than dropped: the author may mean a specific EconLit-linked full-text
source, not EconLit itself as a byte host.

bench/fixtures/README.md's admitted-archive table and per-record table gain
the Wikipedia row and the replaced Cournot/Depitre/des-Michels sourcing when
child 2 actually executes; not edited in this pass, which records scope
only — no new document is sourced here.

### Archive admission is now a standing test, not a per-archive ruling

Ratified the same day (DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-04): an archive is admitted
when it is robot-open (with the Gallica-vs-HAL nuance the drop above already
established — a challenge solved once and durable passes, one that blocks
every fetch does not), licence-open, reputable, names byte-exact identifiers,
and has existed ten years or more. This closes the EconLit question from
earlier: EconLit itself still fails structurally (no hosted full text), but
CORE and RePEc/EconStor plausibly pass all five legs on documentation and
are candidates for child 2 to probe (leg 1, robot-openness, still needs a
real fetch, not a documentation read) rather than sourcing candidates
requiring their own ruling first. FAOLEX and HAL remain pre-existing,
document-specific exceptions, not reopened by this test.

## Re-sourcing PR #151's 19 documents (ruling 2)
| status | documents | action |
|---|---|---|
| compliant | Johnson, Baudelaire, Stein, Curie, Einstein-Minkowski (archive.org) | keep; record identifier + hash |
| archive named, fetched by key | Cournot (Gallica — **dropped 2026-09-04 with Gallica itself, needs a different archive**), Walras, Porte, Minkowski (Internet Archive), Decision 11/2017 (FAOLEX `LEX-FAOC179224`, admitted 2026-09-02: docs/pdf URL, hash, LEX id, Wayback date) | record identifier, re-fetch from the archive, compare hash |
| library-only or non-archive host | Malynes, Ramsey (full first editions on archive.org), Depitre (Gallica — **dropped 2026-09-04 with Gallica itself**), Neurath (dropped: no admitted archive holds it); Circulars 25/2016, 41/2010, 42/2010 (dropped: Wikisource holds no circular as text; the 1992 constitution, vi + en, is the administrative register); Habilitation (HAL, versioned); Highway Code (dropped: no dated UK Government Web Archive snapshot could be confirmed) | re-source or drop |

## Vietnamese slice, rebuilt (ruling 5)
- Administrative register: the 1992 constitution on vi.wikisource, pinned by
  `oldid`; Wikisource holds no ministerial circular as text (checked 2026-09-02).
- Scientific register: 2 HAL records in Vietnamese (ART, COMM), versioned;
  HAL serves a challenge page to scripts, so their bytes are pinned by hand.
- Cross-lingual anchors for R29: des Michels' Lục Vân Tiên and Kim Vân Kiều
  (Gallica, FR–VN in one scan — **dropped 2026-09-04 with Gallica itself;
  this anchor needs a substitute FR–VN parallel text or a different anchor
  shape, open**); a constitution in vi.wikisource and its English text in
  en.wikisource (EN–VN); Bonet 1899 (Internet Archive, VN–FR dictionary,
  pairs with Johnson); Decision 11/2017 with its official English
  translation, from FAOLEX (admitted 2026-09-02, vbpl.vn not).
- Same work in two shapes: Việt Nam sử lược as a Commons scan and as a
  Wikisource transcription.
- Chinese-script bonus for the SHOULD tier: Đại Việt sử ký toàn thư's original.
- Out: Internet Archive's Vietnamese Marx/Lenin translations (translators'
  rights); anything from Gallica's 1922–1954 deposit whose author is not
  life+70 clear.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] the representative mix records each injected Zotero item type and whether
      it is bibliographically correct; intentional errors name the canonical
      type and rationale
- [ ] redundant books are traded for one article in each SHOULD language
      (Arabic, Russian, Chinese, German, Hindi and Spanish), under the same
      archive, licence and byte-pin rules
- [ ] authentic source formats cover PDF, DjVu, EPUB, DOCX, ODT, RTF, HTML,
      plain text and Markdown where an admitted archive supplies them; no
      conversion is performed to manufacture coverage (ticket 0593 owns the
      exhaustive capability matrix and legacy `.doc` gap)
- [ ] stable work identities and typed relations mark one separate-record
      translation pair, one near-duplicate publication pair, one book/chapter
      pair and one metadata-conflicting duplicate
- [ ] the recipe separates parent-item metadata from attachment provenance and
      carries three two-attachment parents: same text in authentic different
      formats, the same text in different languages, and an article with its
      presentation; attachment roles are explicit and passage evidence retains
      the supplying attachment; D6 selection remains deterministic and the
      fixture pins attachment order, detected language, selected keys and
      same-language sibling skip reasons
- [ ] declared failure controls cover a bad PDF, a missing linked file, a valid
      file with no usable text, lying extension or MIME metadata, stale
      extraction, and inconsistent parent/child structure, beside a
      metadata-only or note-only boundary item; each pins its expected
      degradation and answer-set participation
- [ ] fixture composition is bounded by the pinned passage distribution and
      evidence needs rather than the former parent-record tally; language
      articles still replace redundant books while failure and relationship
      shapes may add the records or attachments they require
- [ ] structural marks pin an answer-bearing table, figure caption, annex or
      appendix, footnote or endnote, multi-column article and equation-heavy
      prose to an attachment and page or section, with the extraction
      expectation and text-versus-visual claim stated
- [ ] annotation fixtures distinguish embedded-PDF, Zotero-database and
      note-copied provenance; cover highlight, underline, text-note, image and
      ink shapes; pin page and annotation-key locators, note paragraphs/lists/
      line breaks, page-only versus annotation-specific links, unavailable
      image export, and an independently edited note surviving source deletion
- [ ] one Project Gutenberg ebook supplies official UTF-8 text, HTML and EPUB
      attachments under one work identity; every file is independently pinned,
      the non-paginated text crosses the stock character cap, and no local
      conversion stands in for an archive-issued format
- [ ] fixture attachments cross both stock Zotero extraction caps named in
      SPEC.md §5.2.8 independently and together; export evidence records total
      and indexed pages and characters, with pinned answers on both sides of
      each boundary
- [ ] every fixture document carries a public archive identifier, a byte hash,
      and a licence basis in the source recipe; none is sourced from a personal
      library (ruling 2); versioned identifiers where the archive offers them
      (ruling 3)
- [ ] the re-sourcing table above executed: 5 recorded, 5 re-fetched, 9
      re-sourced or dropped, each outcome logged (recipe landed in PR #177;
      9 entries still await fetch and hash: 8 challenge responses and one
      deliberately capped large file)
- [ ] the Vietnamese MUST tier rebuilt from admitted archives per the slice
      above, with no self-deposit (rulings 4, 5)
- [ ] injection script: collection in a public library, linked-file
      attachments, client version and the two indexing preferences recorded
- [ ] export snapshot committed (items JSON + `/fulltext` JSON per attachment),
      with the re-pin procedure documented as re-export, diff as review artifact
- [ ] Zotero-free index-build harness in bench/ replaying the export through
      0026's mock local API
- [ ] a cross-lingual slice: EN and FR queries whose pinned answer sets are
      Vietnamese entries, gated separately from the monolingual queries so a
      regression names which of R7 and R29 it broke (R29 ratified 2026-08-31;
      SPEC.md §5.2.8 owns the thresholds, this ticket pins them)
- [ ] 200 queries with pinned answer sets (the revised tally above,
      superseding the earlier ~40 and ≈97 figures), each labelled by facet,
      language lane and carrying signal (exact string / paraphrase /
      agreement); no difficulty tiers; each document anchors at least 5
      questions, no ceiling
- [ ] 0026's golden gate consumes it in make check
- [ ] R33's three probe shapes present: a rare exact string, a paraphrase sharing
      no content word with its answer, and an agreement case
- [ ] R34's reading supported: every pinned answer inside the first ten, absolute,
      beside the stability reading of the same sets
- [ ] the intersections carried, not one axis at a time: a monster in a non-Latin
      script, and a scale run at the multilingual default
- [ ] a group-library slice in a closed group, per the perimeter ruling; no trash
      and no feed slice
- [ ] passage-length distribution pinned beside the export (SPEC.md §5.2.8)
- [ ] SPEC.md §5.2.8's fixture description updated to the three-layer shape

